etes with and modifies recrystallization kinetics\",\"explanation\":\"Recrystallization kinetics describe the time-dependent progress of recrystallization after incubation in plain C and Nb steels. The supplied evidence states that Nb substantially affects incubation time, while subsequent kinetics are less strongly affected; niobium solute drag and precipitation nevertheless retard or modify recrystallization as it proceeds. [Data: Entities (429, 464); Relationships (620, 624)]\\n\\nPRECIPITATION KINETICS is explicitly connected to recrystallization kinetics through comparisons of precipitation start and finish times with recrystallization behavior. [Data: Entities (482, 429); Relationships (622)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Rolling schedule affects toughness and microstructure\",\"explanation\":\"Conventional rolling is associated with marked toughness deterioration in Nb steel and with a coarse transformed structure when coarse austenite is rolled. This makes rolling conditions an important part of the material-performance relationship. [Data: Entities (74, 84); Relationships (62)]\\n\\nThe supplied description also states that lower-temperature finish rolling can produce a greater toughness improvement than in Nb-free steel, while deformation in the austenite–ferrite two-phase region is associated with separations, recovered ferrite, and changes in Ar3. These observations connect process design with both toughness and phase-structure outcomes. [Data: Entities (84); Relationships (62)]\"},{\"summary\":\"The community includes measurement and modeling tools\",\"explanation\":\"The Zener–Hollomon parameter is used to analyze Nb steel hot-rolling behavior, including a reported relationship between Z and reciprocal recrystallized austenite grain size. The material description notes that reaching a true steady-state deformation regime may be impractical under relevant rolling conditions. [Data: Entities (84); Relationships (357)]\\n\\nAdditional characterization includes the in